WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.530
OmniInteract learns coordinated
whole-body interactions

2
00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:06.816
between two humanoid robots
from human demonstrations.

3
00:00:07.278 --> 00:00:12.783
We tackle two challenges: preserving surface
contacts despite differences in body shape,

4
00:00:12.783 --> 00:00:15.008
and learning motion control
together with partner

5
00:00:15.008 --> 00:00:18.257
coordination as interaction diversity grows.

6
00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:23.757
Surface Contact-Aware Retargeting,
or SCAR, builds dense correspondences

7
00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:25.788
between human and robot surfaces.

8
00:00:25.908 --> 00:00:31.143
It jointly optimizes both humanoids to preserve
contact interfaces and nearby geometry.

9
00:00:31.263 --> 00:00:35.123
Pose, temporal, and stance
terms maintain motion fidelity,

10
00:00:35.123 --> 00:00:38.964
while kinematic constraints support
feasible robot configurations.

11
00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:42.972
Partner-aware residual experts
learn interaction corrections

12
00:00:42.972 --> 00:00:45.047
on top of a frozen SONIC motion prior.

13
00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:48.554
DAgger distills these experts
into one flow-matching policy.

14
00:00:48.674 --> 00:00:52.384
At deployment, the unified
policy adds residual corrections

15
00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:54.190
to the frozen tracker's actions.

16
00:00:55.327 --> 00:00:59.336
Grasping and hugging examples compare
SCAR with prior retargeting methods.

17
00:00:59.456 --> 00:01:02.276
The close-ups show how SCAR preserves intended

18
00:01:02.276 --> 00:01:04.425
surface contacts and the geometry around them.

19
00:01:05.358 --> 00:01:10.789
In simulation, we compare the baseline with
OmniInteract during hugging and partner dance.

20
00:01:11.089 --> 00:01:13.654
These paired views
highlight whole-body motion,

21
00:01:13.654 --> 00:01:17.178
contact alignment, and the
robots' relative positions.

22
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.564
We now move to two physical Unitree G1 robots.

23
00:01:22.684 --> 00:01:26.407
An external push displaces one
robot as it approaches its partner.

24
00:01:26.527 --> 00:01:30.266
The robots adjust their relative
motion and establish hand contact.

25
00:01:30.386 --> 00:01:32.206
The replay shows the disturbance,

26
00:01:32.206 --> 00:01:34.599
followed by recovery of relative pose

27
00:01:34.599 --> 00:01:36.819
and the intended hand contact in this trial.

28
00:01:37.468 --> 00:01:42.116
Boxing and sparring involve changing
relative poses and repeated arm interactions.

29
00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:46.181
The robots coordinate upper-body
motion while maintaining balance,

30
00:01:46.181 --> 00:01:50.283
linking the movement of each humanoid
with the changing position of its partner.

31
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:55.105
Kicking and fencing extend
the motion repertoire.

32
00:01:55.225 --> 00:01:59.405
These examples involve different limb
movements and changes in relative pose,

33
00:01:59.405 --> 00:02:02.035
illustrating the range of
whole-body interactions

34
00:02:02.035 --> 00:02:05.045
performed by the same
framework on physical robots.

35
00:02:06.508 --> 00:02:10.529
Greeting and close-contact interactions
require a coordinated approach.

36
00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:14.959
The robots align their relative pose,
establish the intended contacts,

37
00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:17.914
and continue moving
together, linking individual

38
00:02:17.914 --> 00:02:20.599
motion tracking with
interaction between partners.

39
00:02:21.523 --> 00:02:25.610
Partner dancing combines arm movements
with coordinated turning and stepping.

40
00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:30.150
The robots adjust their relative
configuration as they change facing direction,

41
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.413
bringing whole-body motion and partner
coordination together in one sequence.

42
00:02:36.538 --> 00:02:37.588
OmniInteract.

43
00:02:37.588 --> 00:02:38.890
Thank you for watching.
